## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of on-device NLP under strict resource constraints, multilingual support, and practical privacy considerations—while remaining accessible to beginners.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning inference
- Lightweight multilingual NLP
- Hashed bag-of-words features with linear models
- Privacy-by-design architecture

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal in-browser inference
function hashWord(w) {
  let h = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
    h = (h * 31 + w.charCodeAt(i)) | 0;
  }
  return Math.abs(h);
}

function predict(text, weights, bias, numBuckets = 2048) {
  const features = new Array(numBuckets).fill(0);
  const words = text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
  for (const word of words) {
    const idx = hashWord(word) % numBuckets;
    features[idx] += 1;
  }
  let score = bias;
  for (let i = 0; i < numBuckets; i++) {
    score += features[i] * weights[i];
  }
  return score > 0 ? 'positive' : 'negative';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical on-device NLP with privacy, multilingual support, and simple explanations—core concerns for real-world edge deployments.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference and privacy preservation
- Multilingual model distillation and language detection
- Pipeline: classify then extract slots
- Lightweight explainability for model decisions
- Offline evaluation with latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
function onDeviceInference(text, model) {
  const lang = detectLanguage(text);
  const category = classifier(lang).predict(text);
  const slots = slotTagger(text);
  const explanation = explain(text, category, slots);
  return { category, slots, explanation };
}
```

